lly  what's been happening recently you've  got two pipelines going from the uk  through to europe the uk is acting as a  kind of land bridge so the lng docks  here it goes across the uk and then goes  out through the interconnector and this  other one the baxton pipeline and if you  look at what's happened in terms of  those pipeline flows they've basically  been at max levels recently so pumping  it as fast as they can  but it's not fast enough and those  pipelines aren't basically big enough to  get all of that lng across and the  upshot is we have a lot of lng coming  into this country liquified natural gas  coming in but not enough of it can get  out through to europe and the upshot is  we can't have more gas in this country  knocking around than we really know what  to do with and that's what brings us  back to this chart a lot of gas here no  one's sure what to do with it so the  price is very low right now who would  have thought now at this stage you're  probably asking right how soon until  that's reflected in my bill and here of  course  there's a catch because the energy  companies say that they base their  prices primarily not on these day ahead  markets but on futures markets looking  out the next month or indeed further so  although these prices are down at the  lowest they've been in 18 months when  you look at futures markets well this is  looking at year ahead prices for gas  what investors think is going to happen  in a year's time actually the uk is very  similar to north europe now that could  be wrong it might not be but at the  moment that's what our bills are based  on and that's why despite the fact that  there's so much gas in in the uk despite  the fact that the stock small markets  put it at the lowest price essentially  in 18 months  still we've got very high record high  bills
 

